You are given an integer n. You need to construct a permutation a1, a2, ..., an using integers from 1 to n such that the following condition is satisfied:

a1 mod a2 >= a2 mod a3 >= ... >= an-1 mod an

If multiple valid permutations exist, you may output any of them. It can be shown that a valid permutation always exists for every n >= 2.

Input:
The first line contains the number of test cases t.
Each test case contains a single integer n.

Output:
For each test case, output n space-separated integers representing a valid permutation.

Constraints:
1 <= t <= 100
2 <= n <= 100

Strategy:
There is a simple trick. We just need to realize that since theres all numbers 1 to n if we start at n and go down to all the results n(i) mod n(i-1) will be 1 except the last one which will be zero. Since the results being equal is allowed this is a universal solution.